ue\n- [ ] A task that exhausts its retries is recorded in the dead-letter list.\n- [ ] `deadLetters()` returns those tasks; `'dead-letter'` event fires.\n- [ ] `retry(id)` can requeue a dead/failed task.\n\n## Phase 7 — Dependencies\n- [ ] `add(payload, { dependencies: [ids] })`; task stays `pending` until all deps `completed`.\n- [ ] When the last dependency completes, the dependent becomes eligible.\n- [ ] If a dependency fails/cancels, the dependent fails with a dependency error.\n- [ ] Dependencies declared before the dependency task is added still resolve.\n\n## Phase 8 — Concurrency control\n- [ ] `new Queue(name, { concurrency })` / `setConcurrency(n)` limits simultaneous active tasks.\n- [ ] Default concurrency is 1 (phase 1 FIFO semantics).\n- [ ] At most `concurrency` tasks are `active` at any moment.\n\n## Phase 9 — Progress & cancellation\n- [ ] `ctx.updateProgress(n)` updates `job.progress` and emits `progress`.\n- [ ] `job.cancel()` / `queue.cancel(id)` cancels waiting/delayed/pending/active tasks.\n- [ ] Active handlers receive an `AbortSignal` (`ctx.signal`); cancellation sets status `cancelled`.\n\n## Phase 10 — Recurring tasks\n- [ ] `addRecurring(cronExpr, payload)` creates tasks on a cron schedule (5-field cron).\n- [ ] Cron supports `*`, lists, ranges, and steps; dom/dow OR semantics.\n- [ ] `removeRecurring(key)` stops a schedule; schedules cleared on close.\n\n## Phase 11 — Middleware\n- [ ] `queue.use((ctx, next) => ...)` wraps processing in a Koa-style pipeline.\n- [ ] Middleware can transform `ctx.payload` before `next()` and `ctx.result` after.\n- [ ] Middleware runs in registration order, innermost call is the handler.\n\n## Phase 12 — Graceful shutdown\n- [ ] `close()` stops accepting new tasks (throws `QueueClosedError` on add).\n- [ ] `close()` waits for active tasks to finish; `close({ drain: true })` also drains waiting.\n- [ ] `close({ force: true })` aborts active tasks; `close({ timeout })` force-aborts after timeout.\n- [ ] `Task